HolRefute: counterexample generation for HOL4
=============================================

Quickcheck (exhaustive and random), native narrowing, and a Kodkod-based
model finder, with kernel certification of the counterexamples they find
where replay succeeds.  User documentation is
`Manual/Description/Refute.smd`; this file is the developer orientation.
Contributor rules are in `CLAUDE.md`.

User interface
--------------

`REFUTE_TAC` runs every selected backend, prints the result and leaves the
goal unchanged.  `QUICKCHECK_TAC`, `NARROWING_TAC` and `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC`
narrow the selection to exhaustive/random/narrowing, narrowing only, and the
model finder only.  `REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_...]` applies `config -> config`
updaters, left to right, to the stored configuration of the context it is
applied in (`set_config` replaces it, `with_config` scopes one);
`REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC cfg` uses exactly `cfg`.  `refute_with` and
`refute_goal_with` are the same outside the tactic layer; `apply_updates`
and `current_config` build configurations from update lists.

Backend selection is `upd_search`: `AllBackends`, `QuickcheckBackends`, or
`Only [Exhaustive | Random | Narrowing | ModelFinder | RegisteredBackend s]`.

One call owns one absolute deadline (default 10 s) consumed by
preprocessing, admission, every backend and Kodkodi; cleanup may finish
after it.  Backends run concurrently in a pool local to the call, each
seeing the whole remainder, unless `upd_sequential true`, which runs them
by weight and splits the remainder among those not yet started.  Either
way the search stops at `NoCounterexample` or at a counterexample reaching
the highest certainty any selected backend declares reachable.

QC `size` and model-finder `card` deepen iteratively from an initial window
of 10.  `upd_size` / `upd_card` pin fixed bounds; `upd_iterative_size` /
`upd_iterative_card` restore adaptive mode.  Other controls (`depth`,
`iterations`, `widths`, `bits`, `iter`, `tac_timeout`, ...) are fixed.

Verdicts and trust
------------------

* `NoCounterexample` claims the whole semantic space was covered: finite
  generator spaces under exhaustive QC or narrowing, or a constant or exactly
  finite-scoped model-finder problem.  Anything bounds-relative is `Unknown`
  with a reason.  QC over `:rat`, `:real`, `fmap` or any infinite generator
  never reports it for a quantified goal.
* `certainty` (`Genuine` / `QuasiGenuine` / `Potential`) is the semantic
  strength; `cert` says whether a HOL theorem was built.  They are
  independent: `Genuine` with `cert = NONE` is valid and expected.
* Substrates are untrusted accelerators.  With `certify = true` (default)
  `Refute_Cert` replays a QC hit with computeLib: true discards it, false
  attaches a theorem, stuck leaves the substrate verdict with `cert = NONE`.
  Narrowing replays its case tree through `Refute_Cert_Narrow`.  Model-finder
  values are replayed by `Refute_Cert_Model` (Skolem provenance, bindings,
  bounded synthesis, one-layer cases, single-property induction, tautology,
  Presburger, `REAL_ARITH`); replay is deterministic and fail-closed and
  never weakens the encoding-derived certainty.
* SETTLED, do not revisit without the owner: Refute trusts Kodkodi exactly
  as Nitpick trusts Kodkod and is never stricter.  A model-finder verdict is
  decided by the encoding (`Refute_ModelFinder_Model.fallback_certainty`),
  never by whether a certificate was built.  `max_potential` bounds models of
  the unsound liberal problem only; every model of a sound problem is
  charged to `max_genuine`.  3948a7bed, 2db19131b and 92c24de7e required a
  certificate and were reverted.
* A polymorphic goal never earns `NoCounterexample` on any engine; a
  counterexample found at a monomorphic proxy stands.  Refuting an
  essential existential over an infinite type is out of reach (as in
  Nitpick); the `real` ersatz is rational-valued.
* No oracle tags.  No Refute-created type, constant, theorem or binding may
  survive in the user's theory on any path: the definitions Compute makes
  through `Refute_EvalEnum` are bracketed in a theory snapshot/revert that
  runs on success, failure, timeout and interruption.

Quickcheck
----------

`Refute_QC` compiles a goal to test-plan IR (`Refute_Eval.plan`) which a
registered substrate compiles and runs.  `Auto` tries NativeSML (in-process
SML extraction, `Refute_Extract`), then Compute (computeLib); an explicitly
selected inapplicable substrate reports `Unknown`.  Both substrates consume
one 64-bit PRNG (`rand_next` in `Refute_Eval`; the extracted native code
calls back into it rather than reimplementing it) in the same order, so
`upd_seed (SOME n)` reproduces the candidate stream on either substrate;
changes to generation order must land in both and in the theory together.

Custom generators (`:rat`, `:real`, `Refute_Gen.GenCustom`) run on Compute
only; `fmap` is a generator family (`register_generator_family`) that
NativeSML declines by name.  Compute decides `=` between ground
`FEMPTY`-rooted update chains through `Refute_EvalFmap` (the stock compset
only reduces the identical pair).  Both substrates take every word width
exhaustively, and width `<= 32` or exactly 64 for random draws.

Smart generators (default on) serve exhaustive testing only; random
testing keeps generate-and-guard plans.  They mode-compile `Hol_reln`
premises and Horn-shaped Boolean function equations into CPS enumerators
(`Enum`), and a negated non-recursive relational premise into a complement
`Guard` (`Refute_EvalEnum.negation_condition`).
`upd_allow_function_inversion` (default false, Isabelle parity)
additionally inverts a function's graph from its own equations
(`Refute_SmartGen.infer_graph`); `flatten_rhs` refuses anything but
constructors, variables and self-calls rather than approximate.
`upd_depth` is the per-`Enum` fuel; fuel-bounded exhaustion is always
incomplete.

`upd_instantiate` pins type variables of QC and narrowing; word-width
variables take fcp numerals from `upd_widths`.  `upd_use_subtype true`
transports a typedef-typed variable to its guarded representation (never
certifies).  `upd_finite_functions true` (default) gives narrowing
function inputs as `ffun` update chains, or `cfun` constants for a
function-typed domain.

Model finder
------------

A port of Nitpick: `Refute_ModelFinder_Preproc` (binarization,
constructor destruction, specialisation, boxing), `_Mono` (monotonicity
calculus), `_Scope` (scope enumeration),
`_Rep` (representation choice), `_Nut`/`_Kodkod` (translation to FORL),
`Refute_Forl`/`Refute_ForlSat` (Kodkodi process bridge and SAT selection),
`_Model` (reconstruction and display), `_HOL` (definition harvesting and
unfolding, typedef/quotient/codatatype registries, ersatz table), `_Names`,
`_Peephole`, `_Util`.  Principal options: `upd_card`, `upd_max`,
`upd_specialize`, `upd_box`, `upd_binary_ints`, `upd_bits`,
`upd_star_linear_preds`, `upd_iter` (default `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`),
`upd_bisim_depth`, `upd_finitize`, `upd_whack`, `upd_need`,
`upd_tac_timeout` (0.5 s; `0` skips), `upd_format`, `upd_max_potential`,
`upd_max_genuine`, `upd_merge_type_vars`; `NONE` keys are row fallbacks.

Native carriers: a word of concrete width `w` has exactly `2^w` atoms
(refused above 1024), `:char` 256; both are exact, never scope-searched,
and turn smart binarization off (forcing it refuses by name).  Every word
constant outside the encoded tier is refused by name.  A typedef over
`:num` or `:int` also disables smart binarization.

Only the equations a user wrote are harvested for a function: HOL4
completes a non-exhaustive pattern set with `ARB`, but that completion
stays in the raw definition, which `raw_standard_props` skips for a
constant that has a DefnBase presentation.  A goal whose truth depends
on the completed value can therefore get a spurious counterexample, as
in Nitpick.  A `refute_simp`/`refute_psimp`/`refute_unfold` clause is
keyed on the head constant under its conclusion, looking through a
negation; one with no constant head is dropped with a warning when the
tables are built rather than aborting harvesting.

The `register_*` entry points and `abstract_generator` change Refute's
state in the HOL context, which is not stored with the theory; the
`export_refute_*` sets persist with the theory.  Typedef and
quotient metadata is harvested lazily on first demand;
`harvest_registrations ()` sweeps the ancestry and the current theory
once.  `refuteTheory` supplies ersatz definitions (`wf'`, `wfrec'`,
`card'`, `sum'`, fmap rows, `safe_The`) for second-order or badly-unfolding
constants; a value-position `unknown` in any harvested axiom vetoes
`NoCounterexample` (`unknown_value`, `Refute_ModelFinder_Kodkod.sml`).
Hilbert choice is guarded per occurrence as `if ?x. P x then @P else
unknown` (`unfold_defs_in_term`), a deliberate deviation from Isabelle,
which leaves each `Eps` occurrence unguarded and constrains it only
through the witness-conditional `Eps_psimp`; an inserted guard vetoes
`NoCounterexample`.

Kodkodi setup: Unix only, needs Java, `setsid` and `/bin/sleep`.  Set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to an unpacked `kodkodi-1.5.7` component (from
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/>); Java comes from
`HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`, then `PATH`;
`HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` overrides the whole launcher.  `sat_solver = "smart"`
prefers a configured native or external solver and falls back to SAT4J
(Nitpick's smart never picks a JNI solver by itself: it tries external
solvers, then SAT4J); JNI solvers are offered when their libraries exist
under `jni/<platform>`, and Isabelle's `*_HOME` variables select external
solvers.  No JVM is started at load or build time.

Module layout
-------------

* `Refute.sig`/`Refute.sml`: public facade over `Refute_Core`.
* `Refute_Core`: types, configuration, backend registry, orchestration.
* `Refute_Config`: configuration types and updaters shared by
  `Refute_Core` and the facade.
* `Refute_Session`: Refute's state in the HOL context; one call reads the
  context captured at its entry and commits its cache changes at exit.
* `Refute_RegistrationData`: structural descriptors, typed theorem codecs,
  and ancestry storage; its load hooks never validate or start Refute.
* `Refute_TypeBase`: `TypeBase` accessors that read the call's context.
* `Refute_Gen`, `Refute_SmartGen`: generator derivation; mode inference.
* `Refute_Eval`: substrate seam and PRNG;
  `Refute_EvalCompute`, `Refute_EvalSML`: substrates;
  `Refute_EvalEnum`: shared enumerator compiler;
  `Refute_EvalRat`, `Refute_EvalReal`: built-in generators and compsets;
  `Refute_EvalFmap`: compset decision of ground finite-map equality.
* `Refute_QC`, `Refute_QC_Narrow`, `Refute_Narrow`: planning, execution,
  native narrowing.
* `Refute_Cert`, `Refute_Cert_Narrow`, `Refute_Cert_Model`: certification.
* `Refute_ModelFinder*`, `Refute_Forl*`, `Refute_PropSat`, `Refute_Skolem`:
  the model finder.
* `Refute_Unused`: unused-assumption probes.
* `refuteScript.sml`: support theory (ersatz definitions).
* `tests/`: selftest and fixture theories.  `theory_tests/`: theory-hygiene
  checks.  `examples/`: executable usage examples (documentation, not
  tests).

Only Poly/ML builds this directory.  Plain `Holmake` builds the library.
`HOLSELFTESTLEVEL=1 Holmake` also builds the test-only directories and runs
their selftests; level 2 adds the acceptance suites and theory-hygiene
checks.  `HOLSELFTESTLEVEL=2 Holmake` is the quality gate.

Persistent structural registrations
-----------------------------------

`register_codatatype`, `register_typedef` and `register_quotient` change
only the current Refute context.  Search and `harvest_registrations ()`
never write structural metadata into a theory.  Theory authors can instead
call `export_codatatype`, `export_typedef` or `export_quotient` with the
same record accepted by the corresponding registration operation.  An
export validates the description and stores the original types, constants
and supporting theorems in the current theory.  Importing that theory makes
its descriptions available to Refute, which validates them on first use.
Derived encoding flags are recomputed; supporting theorems need not have
public DB names.  Codatatype constructor order and the optional witness are
preserved, including the existing trust contract when `witness = NONE`.

For example, after defining a typedef and its bijection laws in a script:

    Refute.export_typedef
      {ty = ``:'a my_type``, abs = ``my_abs``, rep = ``my_rep``,
       absrep_thms = [my_bijections]};

Both a conjunction theorem and a list of its two halves are supported.
As with session registration, type arguments must be distinct variables
(or absent).  A generic description applies at every concrete instance.
Typedef replay still requires the type's `<tyop>_TY_DEF` theorem.

`export_registrations [``:'a old_type``, ``:another_type``]` exports exactly
those operators.  It uses retained inputs from an earlier registration or
harvest, otherwise attempting the ordinary quotient-then-typedef discovery
for each selection.  It does not sweep ancestry.  Repeated operators are
deduplicated in input order; `[]` does nothing.  Every selection must have
an exportable description: failure exports none, and scratch discovery
changes no live harvest caches.  Frac and fmap have no persistent format.
Built-in codata without retained inputs needs an explicit codatatype record.

A wrapper theory may export descriptions for types defined in ancestors;
the wrapper owns the metadata, and clients must import the wrapper or a
descendant.  Rebuild the theory containing the export call to change its
compiled metadata.  Refute may be loaded before or after those imports;
metadata alone does not start a solver or load optional rational/real support.

A deliberate session registration takes precedence over a compatible
persistent description, which takes precedence over an earlier harvest.
Same-kind replacements follow AncestryData delta order: ancestors precede
descendants, and later deltas replace earlier ones.  Sibling replacements
follow the framework's ancestry merge order, so sibling descriptions
are not order-independent.  Common diamond ancestors contribute once.
Incompatible kinds are rejected, retaining the existing exception permitting
an explicit Frac registration to replace a typedef or quotient.

Exports require a current, unpinned theory-authoring context and are rejected
inside a running Refute call.  Context snapshots capture descriptions and
replay caches together; restoring a snapshot restores both.  Only explicit
export writes this metadata.  It adds no public theorem bindings, types,
constants, axioms or oracle tags.

A description whose symbols retire is dropped, like an out-of-date theorem;
other descriptions are unaffected.
